          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pat all the seafood completely dry with kitchen paper. Spread on a tray and refrigerate uncovered for 30 minutes to dry out the surface further."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the flour and cornflour together in a wide bowl. Season with black pepper."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil to 180C in a deep saucepan. Use a thermometer to monitor the temperature throughout."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Working in small batches, toss the seafood in the flour mixture and shake off all excess. Slide gently into the oil. Fry for 2 to 3 minutes until pale gold and crisp. The squid rings will curl and the prawns will turn pink."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove with a slotted spoon and drain on kitchen paper. Allow the oil to return to 180C between batches. Serve immediately with lemon wedges."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Coating soggy instead of crisp"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The seafood was not dry enough before frying, or the oil temperature dropped. Dry thoroughly, fry in small batches, and keep the oil at 180C."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Seafood overcooked and rubbery"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Squid and prawns cook in just 2 to 3 minutes at 180C. Remove them as soon as the coating turns pale gold."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Flour coating too thick and pasty"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Shake off all excess flour before frying. You want a very light dusting, not a coating."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fritto misto is beach food. It is eaten from paper cones at seaside stalls all around the Italian coast. Every fishing port has its own version. The selection changes with what the boats bring in that morning. The rule is always the same: very hot oil, very dry fish, very light coating."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}